45. Public Debt: Burden, Management and Economic Growth
Paper: Public Finance (ECNCC303) | Unit: IV | Topic: Burden of public debt, debt burden and future generation, safe limits, debt management, public debt as a regulating instrument, public debt and economic growth
Introduction
Public debt is the accumulated borrowing of the government. It finances deficits, development and emergencies, but raises concerns about burden on future generations and sustainability.
Burden of public debt: classical and Keynesian views
• Classical view (Ricardo, Smith): debt is a burden, as it diverts resources from private investment (crowding out) and requires future taxes. Public spending is wasteful.
• Keynesian (Lerner) view: internal debt is not a burden, as we owe it to ourselves; transfers within the same generation. External debt is a real burden as it transfers resources abroad.
• Ricardian equivalence (Barro): debt-financed spending equals current taxes, since people anticipate future taxes and save more, so debt is neutral.
Debt burden and future generations
1. Capital stock transfer theory (Modigliani): debt crowds out private capital, so future generations inherit a smaller capital stock and lower income. Internal debt does burden the future if it displaces investment.
2. Welfare attitude theory: burden is the loss of welfare, mostly from taxes needed to service debt, which distorts incentives (excess burden), plus the fall in utility from current sacrifice. Interest payments transfer income from taxpayers to bondholders.
3. Intergeneration equity theory (Musgrave, Buchanan): debt shifts the cost of expenditure to future generations; it is fair to finance capital projects by debt (benefits accrue to the future) but not current consumption (the golden rule of public finance).
Safe limits of public debt
Debt-GDP ratio, interest-revenue ratio, debt-service ratio; sustainability when growth rate exceeds the interest rate (r < g) or when primary surplus covers the gap. India's FRBM Act sets targets for deficit and debt; EU's Maastricht criteria 60 per cent debt and 3 per cent deficit. Reinhart-Rogoff suggest growth slows above 90 per cent, though this is contested.
Public debt management
Objectives: minimise cost subject to risk, extend maturity, develop the bond market, avoid rollover risk. Tools: debt restructuring, conversion, refunding, sinking funds, surplus budgets, and debt-issuance calendars. In India: RBI manages the government's debt; Public Debt Management Cell and the proposed independent debt office.
Public debt as a means of regulating the economy
Debt and open market operations can absorb liquidity during inflation and inject funds during recession, so debt serves as an instrument of compensatory finance and monetary control.
Public debt and economic growth
Productive borrowing for infrastructure raises growth; excess debt raises interest rates, crowds out private investment, and raises default risk. The relationship is likely non-linear, with a threshold.
Conclusion
Debt is neither inherently good nor bad; its effect depends on use, source, level and management.